     ],
     "explanation": "Sensations use 'to be' + adjective."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "a1-present-simple",
   "title": "Present Simple",
   "level": "A1",
   "unit": "Everyday Life",
   "summary": "Habits, routines and facts: I work, she works.",
   "explanation": "Use the present simple for habits, routines, timetables and things that are always true: 'I drink coffee every morning.' 'Water boils at 100 °C.'\nForm: I/you/we/they work · he/she/it works (add -s). Spelling: watch → watches, study → studies, go → goes, have → has.\nNegative: don't / doesn't + base verb: 'She doesn't work on Fridays' (NOT *doesn't works).\nQuestions: Do / Does + subject + base verb: 'Do you speak French?' 'Where does he live?'\nCommon time words: always, usually, often, sometimes, never, every day, on Mondays. Frequency adverbs go before the main verb: 'I usually take the bus.'",
   "explanation_fr": "N'oubliez pas le -s à la 3e personne : she works. Après does/doesn't, le verbe reste à la base : Does she work?",
   "examples": [
    {
     "en": "I start work at nine.",
     "note": "Routine."
    },
    {
     "en": "He lives in Ottawa and works in a bank.",
     "note": "Third person -s."
    },
    {
     "en": "We don't watch TV in the evening.",
     "note": "Negative."
    },
    {
     "en": "Does she speak English? — Yes, she does.",
     "note": "Question."
    }
   ],
