   "summary": "Front negative adverbials and invert subject and auxiliary for formal emphasis.",
   "explanation": "After certain negative or restrictive adverbials placed at the start of a clause, English inverts the subject and auxiliary, as in a question: Never, Rarely, Seldom, Hardly…when, No sooner…than, Not only…but also, Only then, Only after, Under no circumstances, At no time, Little (did I know).\n\n'Never have I seen such volatility.' 'Not only did the company miss its targets, but it also lost its CEO.'\n\nIf there is no auxiliary, add do/does/did. With 'Only after/when/if', inversion happens in the main clause: 'Only after the audit did we discover the error.'\n\nInverted conditionals drop 'if': 'Had we known…' (= If we had known), 'Should you need help…' (= If you need help), 'Were the rates to rise…'. These are very common in formal business writing.",
