   "summary": "Avoid repetition like a native speaker: so, not, one(s), do so, and leaving words out.",
   "explanation": "Native speakers rarely repeat whole phrases. They substitute or omit.\nSubstitution: one/ones for countable nouns ('I prefer the blue one'); do/does/did for verb phrases ('She works harder than I do'); do so in formal style ('The board was asked to reconsider, and it did so'); so/not for clauses after think, hope, expect, suppose, be afraid ('Will rates rise? — I expect so / I hope not').\nEllipsis (leaving out words recoverable from context): after auxiliaries ('I haven't finished, but Sarah has.'), after 'to' ('I'd like to come, but I'm not able to.'), in comparisons ('He earns more than I.' formal / 'than me' informal), and in coordinated clauses ('Some invested in stocks, others in bonds.').\nFrench speakers often say '*I think yes' (je pense que oui) → 'I think so'; '*I hope' alone → 'I hope so'.",
   "explanation_fr": "« Je pense que oui / non » = I think so / I don't think so. « J'espère » en réponse = I hope so.",
   "examples": [
    {
     "en": "Will the deal close this week? — I don't think so.",
     "note": "so after think."
    },
    {
     "en": "You said you'd call, but you didn't.",
     "note": "Ellipsis after auxiliary."
    },
    {
     "en": "Is the report ready? — I'm afraid not.",
     "note": "not after be afraid."
    },
    {
     "en": "Applicants were invited to resubmit, and many did so.",
     "note": "Formal 'do so'."
    }
   ],
   "exercises": [
    {
     "id": "el1",
     "type": "correct",
     "prompt": "Is she coming to the conference? — I think yes.",
     "answer": [
      "I think so.",
      "I think so",
      "Is she coming to the conference? — I think so."
     ],
     "explanation": "think so (not 'think yes')."
    },
    {
     "id": "el2",
     "type": "mcq",
     "prompt": "Are prices going to rise again? — I hope ___.",
     "options": [
      "no",
      "not",
      "don't",
      "nothing"
     ],
     "answer": "not",
     "explanation": "hope not = I hope they won't."
    },
    {
     "id": "el3",
     "type": "gap",
     "prompt": "I wanted to attend the webinar, but I wasn't able ___.",
     "answer": [
      "to"
     ],
     "explanation": "Ellipsis after 'to'."
    },
    {
     "id": "el4",
     "type": "mcq",
     "prompt": "These reports are better than the ___ we received last year.",
     "options": [
      "one",
      "ones",
      "those ones",
      "it"
     ],
     "answer": "ones",
     "explanation": "Plural substitute for 'reports'."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "quantifiers",
   "title": "Quantifiers and Determiners",
   "level": "B2",
   "unit": "Determiners",
   "summary": "much/many, few/a few, little/a little, each/every, either/neither, all/whole.",
   "explanation": "Countable plural: many, (a) few, fewer, a number of. Uncountable: much, (a) little, less, an amount of. Both: a lot of, plenty of, some, any, no, most.\n'A few / a little' = some (positive); 'few / little' = not enough (negative): 'Few candidates passed' (disappointing) vs 'A few candidates passed' (some did).\n'Each' looks at members individually (often two or more); 'every' at the whole group (three or more): 'Each partner signed.' 'Every employee received a bonus.'\n'Either/neither' for two: 'Neither option is ideal.' 'All' vs 'whole': 'all the day' is unnatural → 'the whole day', 'all day'.\nIn formal English, use 'fewer' with countables: 'fewer errors' (not *less errors).",
   "explanation_fr": "« Peu de » = few/little ; « quelques » = a few. « Moins de » + dénombrable = fewer.",
   "examples": [
    {
     "en": "We received fewer complaints this quarter.",
     "note": "fewer + countable."
    },
    {
     "en": "There's little evidence to support the claim.",
     "note": "little = almost none."
    },
    {
     "en": "Each of the three options has drawbacks.",
     "note": "each + singular verb."
    },
    {
     "en": "I spent the whole afternoon on the report.",
     "note": "the whole + singular noun."
    }
   ],
   "exercises": [
    {
     "id": "qn1",
     "type": "mcq",
     "prompt": "There were ___ questions after the talk, so we finished early.",
     "options": [
      "few",
      "a few",
      "little",
      "a little"
     ],
     "answer": "few",
     "explanation": "Negative meaning: not many."
    },
    {
     "id": "qn2",
     "type": "mcq",
     "prompt": "We have ___ time left: let's take a short break.",
     "options": [
      "a little",
      "a few",
      "few",
      "many"
     ],
     "answer": "a little",
     "explanation": "Uncountable, positive meaning."
    },
    {
     "id": "qn3",
     "type": "correct",
     "prompt": "There were less people at the conference than last year.",
     "answer": [
      "There were fewer people at the conference than last year."
     ],
     "explanation": "people is countable → fewer."
    },
    {
     "id": "qn4",
     "type": "mcq",
     "prompt": "___ of the two proposals was accepted; both were rejected.",
     "options": [
      "Either",
      "Neither",
      "None",
      "Both"
     ],
     "answer": "Neither",
     "explanation": "Two items, negative → neither."
    },
    {
     "id": "qn5",
     "type": "correct",
     "prompt": "I worked all the day on the presentation.",
     "answer": [
      "I worked all day on the presentation.",
      "I worked the whole day on the presentation."
     ],
     "explanation": "all day / the whole day."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "causative",
   "title": "Causatives: have/get something done",
   "level": "B2",
   "unit": "Verb Patterns",
   "summary": "Arranging for others to do things, and 'have/get someone do/to do'.",
   "explanation": "have/get + object + past participle = arrange for someone else to do it: 'We had the accounts audited.' 'I need to get my laptop repaired.'\nAlso for unpleasant experiences: 'He had his wallet stolen.'\nhave + person + infinitive (without to): 'I'll have my assistant send you the file.'\nget + person + to-infinitive (persuade): 'I got the bank to waive the fee.'\nmake + person + infinitive (force): 'They made us sign a confidentiality agreement.'\nlet + person + infinitive (allow): 'My manager let me work from home.'",
   "explanation_fr": "« Faire faire » = have/get something done : « J'ai fait réparer ma voiture » = I had my car repaired.",
   "examples": [
    {
     "en": "We're having the office repainted next week.",
     "note": "Arranged service."
    },
    {
     "en": "She got her colleague to cover the meeting.",
     "note": "Persuaded."
    },
    {
     "en": "He had his car broken into last night.",
     "note": "Unpleasant experience."
    },
    {
     "en": "The auditor made us provide every receipt.",
     "note": "Obligation."
    }
   ],
   "exercises": [
    {
     "id": "ca1",
     "type": "translate",
     "prompt": "Traduisez : « Je fais réviser mon CV par un professionnel. »",
     "answer": [
      "I'm having my CV reviewed by a professional.",
      "I am having my CV reviewed by a professional.",
      "I'm getting my CV reviewed by a professional.",
      "I have my CV reviewed by a professional.",
      "I'm having my resume reviewed by a professional.",
      "I'm having my résumé reviewed by a professional."
     ],
     "explanation": "have/get + object + past participle."
    },
    {
     "id": "ca2",
     "type": "mcq",
     "prompt": "I finally got the landlord ___ the heating.",
     "options": [
      "fix",
      "to fix",
      "fixed",
      "fixing"
     ],
     "answer": "to fix",
     "explanation": "get + person + to-infinitive."
    },
    {
     "id": "ca3",
     "type": "mcq",
     "prompt": "They made the interns ___ late every night.",
     "options": [
      "stay",
      "to stay",
      "staying",
      "stayed"
     ],
     "answer": "stay",
     "explanation": "make + person + bare infinitive."
    },
    {
     "id": "ca4",
     "type": "correct",
     "prompt": "I need to repair my phone by a technician.",
     "answer": [
      "I need to have my phone repaired by a technician.",
      "I need to get my phone repaired by a technician."
     ],
     "explanation": "The technician repairs it → causative."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "future-in-the-past",
   "title": "Future in the Past",
   "level": "C1",
   "unit": "Tenses",
   "summary": "was going to, would, was about to, was to: talking about the future from a past viewpoint.",
   "explanation": "Use these forms to describe what was planned, expected or predicted at a past moment.\nwas/were going to: plans that often did not happen: 'I was going to call you, but I forgot.'\nwould: predictions/reported futures: 'She knew the project would succeed.'\nwas/were about to: something on the point of happening: 'We were about to sign when the deal collapsed.'\nwas/were to (formal): destiny or arrangements: 'He was to become CEO ten years later.' 'The meeting was to have taken place on Monday' (but didn't).\nwas/were (just) on the point of + -ing: 'I was on the point of leaving.'",
   "explanation_fr": "« J'allais t'appeler » = I was going to call you. « Il allait devenir PDG » (destin) = He was to become CEO.",
   "examples": [
    {
     "en": "I was going to apply, but the deadline had passed.",
     "note": "Unfulfilled plan."
    },
    {
     "en": "Nobody imagined the company would grow so fast.",
     "note": "Past prediction."
    },
    {
     "en": "They were about to launch when the regulator intervened.",
     "note": "Imminent."
    },
    {
     "en": "Little did she know she was to lead the firm one day.",
     "note": "Formal 'destiny'."
    }
   ],
   "exercises": [
    {
     "id": "fp1",
     "type": "mcq",
     "prompt": "I ___ email you, but I lost your address.",
     "options": [
      "will",
      "was going to",
      "am going to",
      "would have"
     ],
     "answer": "was going to",
     "explanation": "Unfulfilled past plan."
    },
    {
     "id": "fp2",
     "type": "mcq",
     "prompt": "We ___ leave when the fire alarm went off.",
     "options": [
      "were about to",
      "are about to",
      "will be about to",
      "had been about to"
     ],
     "answer": "were about to",
     "explanation": "On the point of happening in the past."
    },
    {
     "id": "fp3",
     "type": "translate",
     "prompt": "Traduisez : « Il savait que le marché allait se redresser. »",
     "answer": [
      "He knew the market would recover.",
      "He knew that the market would recover.",
      "He knew the market was going to recover.",
      "He knew that the market was going to recover."
     ],
     "explanation": "Future seen from the past → would / was going to."
    },
    {
     "id": "fp4",
     "type": "mcq",
     "prompt": "The merger ___ completed in March, but it was postponed.",
     "options": [
      "was to have been",
      "will have been",
      "is to be",
      "would be"
     ],
     "answer": "was to have been",
     "explanation": "was to + perfect passive infinitive = planned but didn't happen."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "discourse-markers",
   "title": "Discourse Markers for Speaking and Writing",
   "level": "C1",
   "unit": "Cohesion",
   "summary": "however, nevertheless, whereas, as a result, that said, admittedly: linking ideas precisely.",
   "explanation": "Contrast: however, nevertheless, nonetheless (formal, sentence-linking); whereas, while (contrast two clauses); that said / having said that (spoken, concession after a point); even so.\nConcession: admittedly, granted, it is true that… but…\nCause/result: as a result, consequently, therefore, thus (formal), hence (formal, often + noun: 'hence the delay').\nAddition: moreover, furthermore, in addition, what is more.\nReformulation: in other words, that is to say, to put it another way.\nPunctuation: 'However' joining clauses needs a semicolon or full stop: 'Sales rose; however, costs rose faster.' 'Whereas' joins clauses directly: 'Sales rose, whereas costs fell.'\nFrench traps: 'actually' ≠ actuellement (= currently); 'eventually' ≠ éventuellement (= possibly); 'in fact' is fine; 'at the contrary' → 'on the contrary'.",
   "explanation_fr": "Faux amis : « actually » = en fait ; « eventually » = finalement ; « éventuellement » = possibly ; « au contraire » = on the contrary.",
   "examples": [
    {
     "en": "The plan is ambitious. That said, it is achievable.",
     "note": "Spoken concession."
    },
    {
     "en": "Prices rose sharply; hence the drop in demand.",
     "note": "hence + noun."
    },
    {
     "en": "Admittedly, the data are limited, but the trend is clear.",
     "note": "Concession."
    },
    {
     "en": "Currently, we have two offices.",
     "note": "Not 'actually'."
    }
   ],
   "exercises": [
    {
     "id": "dm1",
     "type": "mcq",
     "prompt": "The first quarter was weak. ___, the year ended strongly.",
     "options": [
      "Nevertheless",
      "Whereas",
      "Because",
      "Moreover"
     ],
     "answer": "Nevertheless",
     "explanation": "Contrast between sentences."
    },
    {
     "id": "dm2",
     "type": "correct",
     "prompt": "Actually, the company employs 500 people, but next year it will employ 800.",
     "answer": [
      "Currently, the company employs 500 people, but next year it will employ 800.",
      "At the moment, the company employs 500 people, but next year it will employ 800.",
      "At present, the company employs 500 people, but next year it will employ 800."
     ],
     "explanation": "actuellement = currently."
    },
    {
     "id": "dm3",
     "type": "mcq",
     "prompt": "The supplier was late; ___ the delay in production.",
     "options": [
      "hence",
      "therefore",
      "so that",
      "thus that"
     ],
     "answer": "hence",
     "explanation": "hence + noun phrase."
    },
    {
     "id": "dm4",
     "type": "correct",
     "prompt": "At the contrary, the results were excellent.",
     "answer": [
      "On the contrary, the results were excellent."
     ],
     "explanation": "on the contrary."
    },
    {
     "id": "dm5",
     "type": "mcq",
     "prompt": "Investors were optimistic, ___ analysts were more cautious.",
     "options": [
      "whereas",
      "however",
      "moreover",
      "therefore"
     ],
     "answer": "whereas",
     "explanation": "Joins two contrasting clauses."
    }
   ]
  }
 ]
}

