   "lines": [
    {
     "s": "Moderator",
     "t": "Our question tonight: can economies keep growing while cutting emissions fast enough to meet climate targets?"
    },
    {
     "s": "Economist",
     "t": "The evidence suggests they can, at least partially. Over thirty countries have grown their economies while reducing emissions, which we call absolute decoupling."
    },
    {
     "s": "Activist",
     "t": "With respect, the pace matters. Decoupling is happening, but nowhere near quickly enough. At current rates, we'd overshoot our targets by decades."
    },
    {
     "s": "Economist",
     "t": "I don't dispute that. My point is that growth itself isn't the enemy. What matters is what we grow: renewable energy, efficient buildings, public transport."
    },
    {
     "s": "Activist",
     "t": "But there's a risk that green growth becomes an excuse for business as usual, as if technology will rescue us without any change in how we consume."
    },
    {
     "s": "Moderator",
     "t": "So is there any common ground?"
    },
    {
     "s": "Economist",
     "t": "Plenty, I'd say. We both want a much higher carbon price and far faster investment in clean infrastructure."
    },
    {
     "s": "Activist",
     "t": "Agreed. Where we differ is on whether rich countries should also aim to consume less. I believe they should."
    }
