  {
   "id": "radio-nudging",
   "title": "Radio Talk: The Ethics of Nudging",
   "level": "C2",
   "kind": "Radio talk",
   "domain": "academic",
   "speakers": {
    "Speaker": {
     "accent": "en-GB",
     "voice": "fable"
    }
   },
   "lines": [
    {
     "s": "Speaker",
     "t": "A nudge, in the jargon of behavioural economics, is any feature of the way a choice is presented that predictably alters behaviour without forbidding any option or significantly changing incentives."
    },
    {
     "s": "Speaker",
     "t": "Placing fruit at eye level in a canteen is a nudge; banning chips is not. Automatically enrolling employees in a pension scheme, while allowing them to opt out, is perhaps the best-known example, and it has dramatically increased saving rates."
    },
    {
     "s": "Speaker",
     "t": "Proponents describe this as libertarian paternalism: steering people towards choices they would endorse on reflection, while leaving them free to choose otherwise."
    },
    {
     "s": "Speaker",
     "t": "Critics are unconvinced. If nudges work precisely because people do not notice them, the objection goes, they bypass rather than respect our capacity for reason."
    },
    {
     "s": "Speaker",
     "t": "There is also the awkward question of who decides which direction we are nudged in, and on what authority. A nudge towards saving seems benign; a nudge towards a particular political view would not."
    },
    {
     "s": "Speaker",
     "t": "My own view is that the defence of nudging depends heavily on transparency. A nudge that would still work if it were openly disclosed is, I think, far easier to justify than one that relies on our not knowing it is there."
    }
   ],
   "questions": [
    {
     "id": "q1",
     "prompt": "Which of these is NOT a nudge, according to the definition?",
