            {
              "id": "academic", "type": "mcq", "title": "Read an Academic Passage",
              "passage": "The domestication of plants did not happen in a single place or at a single moment. Archaeological evidence indicates that agriculture emerged independently in at least a dozen regions, including the Fertile Crescent, China, Mesoamerica, the Andes and parts of Africa, over a period of several thousand years. In each case, people gradually began to favour plants with useful traits, such as seeds that remained on the stalk instead of scattering, which made harvesting easier.\n\nFor a long time, scholars assumed that farming spread because it obviously improved human life. Skeletal remains complicate this picture. Early farming populations often show signs of poorer nutrition and more dental disease than the hunter-gatherers who preceded them, probably because their diets relied heavily on a few starchy crops. Why, then, did agriculture spread? One explanation is demographic: farming produces more food per area of land, even if that food is less varied, allowing populations to grow. Once a community had committed to farming and its numbers had increased, returning to hunting and gathering was no longer a realistic option.",
              "questions": [
                {"id": "q1", "prompt": "What does the passage say about where agriculture began?", "options": ["It began in the Fertile Crescent and spread everywhere.", "It emerged independently in many regions.", "It began in Africa.", "Its origins are completely unknown."], "answer": "It emerged independently in many regions.", "explanation": "'independently in at least a dozen regions'."},
                {"id": "q2", "prompt": "Why were seeds that stayed on the stalk useful?", "options": ["They grew faster.", "They made harvesting easier.", "They were more nutritious.", "They resisted disease."], "answer": "They made harvesting easier.", "explanation": "Paragraph 1."},
