             "passage": "There is a particular kind of silence that descends on a concert hall in the moments before the music begins — not the absence of sound, exactly, but a collective holding of breath. I have spent much of my career trying to understand what happens in that silence, and I am no closer to an answer than when I began.\n\nThe conventional explanation is that the audience is simply waiting, and that the silence is a courtesy, a clearing of space. But that strikes me as too modest. The silence is not merely the frame around the performance; it is part of it. Anyone who has heard a great conductor wait, seemingly interminably, before the first note knows that the tension generated in those seconds shapes everything that follows.\n\nThis is, I suspect, why recordings, for all their technical perfection, so often disappoint. They begin, as it were, mid-sentence. The silence that precedes them is the silence of a living room or a car, a silence that belongs to the listener alone and therefore carries no shared expectation. We have gained the ability to hear any music at any time, and lost, without quite noticing, the communal anticipation that once gave it weight.\n\nI do not wish to romanticise the concert hall. It is an institution with its own absurdities, from the ritual coughing between movements to the anxious etiquette of applause. Nor am I suggesting that we abandon recorded music, which has brought extraordinary works to millions who would otherwise never have heard them. My point is narrower: that something is lost when music becomes a commodity always within reach, and that what is lost is precisely what cannot be recorded.",
